course: dictionary
lesson: 704
title: A name that becomes a word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words start as names of people, and then they grow into words for a whole group. Baptist is one of them. Read the context first: «Baptists and many Pentecostal churches baptise people old enough to decide, usually by full immersion, which means going right under the water.» The sentence tells you that Baptists are churches that baptise people, so a Baptist is a member of one of those churches.
explain: A capital letter is a clue. In «John the Baptist», the capital B shows it is part of a name. He was a prophet who baptised people in the river Jordan, including Jesus, and his name tells us what he did.
explain: Baptist can also be an adjective, a word that describes a noun. In «a Baptist chapel», the word tells you which church the chapel belongs to. Ask yourself what the word describes, and check that your meaning still fits the sentence.
fact: A Baptist is a member of a Protestant Christian church.
fact: Baptist churches baptise only people old enough to say they believe, usually by full immersion, which means putting them fully under the water.
fact: John the Baptist was the prophet who baptised Jesus in the river Jordan.
fact: Baptist churches are common in the USA (especially in the South), in Wales and in Australia.
fact: Each Baptist church usually runs its own affairs.
exercise: «Baptists and many Pentecostal churches baptise people old enough to decide.» Which people do these churches baptise? | *People old enough to decide | Babies | Only the church leaders | Animals
exercise: What is John the Baptist known for? | *Baptising people in the river Jordan, including Jesus | Building churches in Wales | Ruling a big kingdom | Writing the Bible alone
exercise: In «a Baptist chapel», what does Baptist describe? | The colour of the walls | *The church the chapel belongs to | The size of the chapel | The number of seats
test: In «a Baptist chapel», what part of speech is Baptist? | Verb | *Adjective | Noun | Adverb
test: «Baptists baptise people usually by full immersion.» What does immersion mean? | Being dried with a towel | *Going right under the water | Being tickled | Standing in the rain
exam: Using the sentence «Herod kept a wicked oath, and it cost John the Baptist his life», what can you work out? | He became a king | *He was killed | He wrote a book | He lived on a mountain forever
